/**
 * Normalizes Contact phone numbers for the new dialer.
 *
 * The dialer only accepts digits and a leading '+', so every other character
 * is stripped from Contact.Phone. Contacts without a phone are ignored.
 */
public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    /** Matches every character that is not an ASCII digit and not '+'. */
    private static final Pattern NON_DIALABLE = Pattern.compile('[^0-9+]');

    /**
     * Strips every character that is not a digit or '+' from the Phone of the
     * given contacts and saves the changed records in one partial-success DML.
     *
     * @param contactIds Contacts to clean up (up to 200 per call).
     * @return Id of every contact that could not be saved -> its first error
     *         message. Empty when every record was saved.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls, so the query stays cheap and no record
        // can be handed to DML twice.
        Set<Id> ids = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id contactId : contactIds) {
            if (contactId != null) {
                ids.add(contactId);
            }
        }
        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // Collect only the contacts that actually have a phone; the rest are
        // left out of the DML entirely.
        List<Contact> contacts = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact contact : [SELECT Id, Phone FROM Contact WHERE Id IN :ids]) {
            if (String.isBlank(contact.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }
            contact.Phone = NON_DIALABLE.matcher(contact.Phone).replaceAll('');
            contacts.add(contact);
        }

        if (contacts.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // allOrNone = false: one bad record does not roll back the others.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(contacts, false);

        // Save results come back in the same order as the input list.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult result = results[i];
            if (result.isSuccess()) {
                continue;
            }
            List<Database.Error> errors = result.getErrors();
            failures.put(
                contacts[i].Id,
                errors.isEmpty() ? 'Unknown error' : errors[0].getMessage()
            );
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

## What I changed in your approach, and why

1. **Only contacts with a phone go into the DML list.** Your wording could be read as "save all queried contacts". Sending records you didn't touch to `Database.update` still burns DML rows, still fires triggers/flows/validation rules, and can produce failures that have nothing to do with the phone cleanup. Contacts without a phone are simply never added to the list.

2. **Blank is treated the same as null.** `String.isBlank(c.Phone)` also catches `'   '`, which sanitizes to `''` — that's "no phone", not a phone worth saving.

3. **The input Ids go through a `Set<Id>` first.** If the caller ever passes the same Id twice, a `List`-based version would put the same record into one DML call twice (which throws) or update it twice. A Set also lets me drop nulls cheaply. This doesn't change the result for well-formed input.

4. **Failures are keyed by `contacts[i].Id`, not by `result.getId()`.** `SaveResult.getId()` is reliable for updates, but pairing by index is guaranteed correct because save results are returned in input order, and it makes the intent obvious to the next reader. I also guard against an empty `getErrors()` array instead of blindly indexing `[0]`.

5. **`public with sharing`.** The class reads and writes Contact records on behalf of whoever calls it, so record-level sharing should be enforced by default. If you need it to run in system context (e.g. an admin batch), change the declaration to `without sharing` or `inherited sharing` — but don't leave it unspecified.

6. **A statically compiled `Pattern`.** `Pattern.compile` runs once per transaction instead of once per record via `String.replaceAll`. Cosmetic at 200 records, free at any scale. `[^0-9+]` deliberately keeps only ASCII digits, so Unicode digits (Arabic-Indic, full-width, etc.) are stripped — what a dialer usually wants.

**Things I deliberately did not change:** it's still one SOQL query and exactly one `Database.update(contacts, false)`, and I did not switch to `AccessLevel.USER_MODE` (you'd only want that if callers may lack FLS/edit access, in which case use `Database.update(contacts, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE)` and expect more entries in the failure map). Records that are already clean are still saved, as you asked; skipping them would be a valid optimization but it would silently change what can appear in the failure map. Note also that an input Id that doesn't exist or that the running user can't see simply never comes back from the query, so it can't appear in the failure map — flag that separately if you need it. And a test class is still required for deployment (75% coverage, asserting on a mixed success/failure batch).

